        "section": [
          {
            "text": {
              "status": "generated",
              "div": "<div xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\">COPD exacerbation, treated with bronchodilators and steroids</div>"
            },
            "code": {
              "coding": [
                {
                  "code": "67781-5",
                  "system": "http://loinc.org",
                  "display": "Summarization of encounter note"
                }
              ]
            },
            "title": "Synthesis"
          },
          {
            "text": {
              "status": "generated",
              "div": "<div xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\">Dear colleague, I am writing to inform you about the recent hospital admission of your 77-year-old male patient, who presented with an acute exacerbation of his known chronic obstructive pulmonary disease. On admission, he exhibited increased dyspnoea, productive cough, and reduced oxygen saturation. Chest X-ray confirmed hyperinflation without signs of pneumonia. Arterial blood gas analysis revealed mild hypoxaemia. Blood tests showed elevated inflammatory markers. The patient was treated with nebulised bronchodilators, systemic corticosteroids, and supplemental oxygen therapy, resulting in satisfactory clinical improvement. Pulse oximetry was monitored continuously throughout the stay. The patient was discharged in a stable condition with an adjusted inhaler regimen and a short course of oral corticosteroids. I recommend close follow-up and review of his long-term COPD management plan. Please do not hesitate to contact me should you have any questions.</div>"
            },
            "code": {
